## Database schema (models + Alembic migration)

users
  id (uuid, pk), email (unique), hashed_password, full_name, role (enum: customer, admin), 
  created_at

addresses
  id (uuid, pk), user_id (fk -> users), label, street, city, state, zip, is_default, 
  created_at

products
  id (uuid, pk), name, category (enum: dairy, eggs, pantry, other), description, 
  unit_price, is_available, created_at

subscriptions
  id (uuid, pk), user_id (fk), pickup_location, frequency (enum: weekly, biweekly, monthly), 
  next_delivery_date, status (enum: active, paused, cancelled), created_at, updated_at

subscription_items
  id (uuid, pk), subscription_id (fk), product_id (fk), quantity

orders
  id (uuid, pk), user_id (fk), subscription_id (fk, nullable — null if one-time order), 
  pickup_location, order_date, status (enum: pending, confirmed, ready, picked_up, 
  cancelled), created_at

order_items
  id (uuid, pk), order_id (fk), product_id (fk), quantity

inventory
  id (uuid, pk), product_id (fk), quantity_on_hand, low_stock_threshold, updated_at

deliveries
  id (uuid, pk), delivery_date (must fall on a Wednesday — validate in service layer), 
  pickup_location, time_delivered (nullable), total_items, created_at

delivery_pickups
  id (uuid, pk), delivery_id (fk), order_id (fk), picked_up (bool, default false), 
  picked_up_at (nullable)

Generate an Alembic migration for all of the above, plus a seed script 
(app/seed.py, run manually) that inserts realistic dummy data: 5-6 users, 8-10 products 
across categories, several active/paused subscriptions, a handful of one-time orders, 
inventory levels for each product (some intentionally below threshold to test low-stock 
alerts), and 2-3 delivery records with a mix of picked-up and pending pickups.

## API endpoints (routers)

customers.py
  POST /auth/register, POST /auth/login
  GET /customers/me, PATCH /customers/me
  GET /customers/me/addresses, POST /customers/me/addresses

subscriptions.py
  POST /subscriptions
  GET /subscriptions/{id}
  PATCH /subscriptions/{id}          # modify frequency/items
  POST /subscriptions/{id}/pause
  POST /subscriptions/{id}/resume
  POST /subscriptions/{id}/skip-next

orders.py
  GET /orders                          # current + past, filterable by user
  GET /orders/{id}
  GET /orders/upcoming

products.py
  GET /products                        # filterable by category, searchable by query param
  GET /products/{id}
  GET /products/{id}/availability

inventory.py                           # admin-only (enforce via role dependency)
  GET /inventory
  GET /inventory/low-stock

All routers should raise consistent HTTPException-based errors (404 for not found, 403 
for role violations, 422 for validation) so the agent's tool layer gets predictable, 
deterministic failure states to relay back to the user — this matters for the reliability 
requirement (agent must return deterministic error messages when tools fail).

## Agent layer (agent/tools.py + agent/main.py)

Implement these as Strands @tool functions, calling the service layer directly 
(same process, no HTTP round-trip):

Read-only (build and test these first):
  get_subscription(user_id, subscription_id=None)
  get_order_status(order_id)
  get_next_delivery(user_id)
  search_products(query=None, category=None)
  check_availability(product_id)
  get_inventory_summary()              # admin context only

Mutating (build second, after read-only tools are verified working):
  pause_subscription(subscription_id)
  resume_subscription(subscription_id)
  skip_next_delivery(subscription_id)
  update_subscription(subscription_id, frequency=None, items=None)

Each tool's docstring should be clear and specific (Strands uses it to decide when to 
call the tool) and each should catch exceptions from the service layer and return a 
clean error message string rather than raising, so the agent can relay failures 
conversationally instead of crashing.

agent/main.py: 
  - BedrockModel configured with model_id as an env var (default to a Claude model via 
    Bedrock's inference profile format, e.g. us.anthropic.claude-... — pull the current 
    correct ID format from Strands docs rather than assuming)
  - System prompt describing the Harmony Acres assistant, its scope (subscriptions, 
    orders, products, delivery status), and that it should ask for clarification before 
    calling mutating tools if the user's request is ambiguous (e.g. which subscription, 
    if they have more than one)
  - Register all tools from tools.py
  - Simple terminal loop for local interactive testing (input() / print(), "quit" to exit) 
    — this is how we'll manually test the agent against the real Neon-backed API
    before building any frontend

